<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Camulodunum</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Camulodunum, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colchester">Colchester</a> in Essex, was the chief centre of the Trinovantes and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunobelinus">Cunobelinus</a>, a vast oppidum protected by long earthwork dykes. After the Roman invasion of 43 CE it became the first capital of Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and a colony of veterans with a temple to the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boudica">Boudica</a>'s rebels destroyed it in 60 or 61, and it was rebuilt with stone walls that still survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/camulodunum.jpg" alt="Camulodunum" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maria · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Roman_Town_Wall,_Head_Street_to_the_Balkerne_Gate_3.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Camulodunon, the stronghold of Camulos, a British god of war, is named on coins minted by the chieftain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tasciovanus">Tasciovanus</a> between 20 and 10 BCE. Its earthworks, begun in the 1st century BCE but mostly of the 1st century CE, are considered the most extensive of their kind in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. The River Colne guarded the site to the north and east and the Roman River valley to the south, and the dykes closed the western gap, enclosing 1,000 ha. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunobelinus">Cunobelinus</a> it replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verulamium">Verlamion</a> as the most important settlement of pre-Roman Britain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cunobelinus">Cunobelinus</a> died around 40 CE, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caratacus">Caratacus</a> ruled from Camulodunon. In 43 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aulus-plautius">Aulus Plautius</a> led four legions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> with the town as their main target, and the Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> arrived with reinforcements, including artillery and elephants, to lead the attack. Caratacus fled to the Ordovices and Silures in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. The first permanent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress in Britain was built within the oppidum for the Twentieth Legion, and the colony that followed held the province's only classical-style temple, the centre of the Imperial Cult.</p><p>Rebuilt after the revolt, the town, officially Colonia Claudia Victricensis, grew to 45 ha and flourished in the 2nd and 3rd centuries, though the procurator's office moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/londinium">Londinium</a>. Its walls, completed by 80, were the first town walls in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and their Balkerne Gate is still Britain's largest Roman gateway. The town had two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a>, Britain's only known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> circus and more than 50 mosaics, was for a time the province's only producer of samian ware, and may have reached a population of 30,000.</p><p>In the crises of the late 3rd century the Balkerne Gate and Duncan's Gate were blocked. A church at Butt Road, dated from 320 to about 425, may be one of the earliest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Life continued at a much reduced level after Roman administration collapsed in 409–411. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colchester">Colchester</a> re-entered the written record in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> for 917, when King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-elder">Edward the Elder</a> retook it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Roman walls survive to a height of over 6 m, part of the precinct wall of the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> can be seen near the castle, which alone used over 25,000 cubic metres of reused Roman tile and brick, and the Norman chapel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helena">St Helena</a> stands in a corner of the Roman theatre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Camulodunum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cunobelinus, ruling from about 9 to 40 CE from <b>Camulodunum</b>, which he took from the Trinovantes, extended influence over Kent and much of the south-east; Suetonius called him king of the Britons.</blockquote><a href="/state/catuvellauni">Catuvellauni</a></li><li><blockquote>Rulers such as Addedomarus and Dubnovellaunus issued gold coins, and <b>Camulodunum</b> grew into a vast oppidum defended by long dykes, with burials like the Lexden tumulus holding Roman luxury goods.</blockquote><a href="/state/trinovantes">Trinovantes</a></li></ul></section>
